BSim VT correlator: top-K truncation of per-query candidate pairs (non-lossy
alternative to the Tier-1 empty-set candidate cap)

Date: 2026-06-10
Applies to: upstream/fork ghidra master (tested against the milohax fork at
            /home/free/code/milohax/ghidra, files unmodified on master)
Apply with: git apply bsim-topk-cap.patch   (or patch -p1)
Compile-checked: javac --release 21 against the fork dist jars under
            build/ghidra-dist/ghidra_12.2_DEV/ (whole vt/api package compiled
            together; BinningSystem is package-private). Class files verified
            in /tmp/bsim-topk/classes.

PROBLEM
  ghidriff --bsim on RB3 Wii Bank8 (40k funcs) vs stripped Xbox360 Xenon
  (65k funcs) stalls 70+ minutes single-threaded after decompilation: degenerate
  LSH bins make BinningSystem.lookup() return O(n_source) candidates per
  destination query, and MatchingCallback.findSimilarNodes adds EVERY pair
  passing the similarity threshold, producing an O(n_src x n_dest) FunctionPair
  blowup in the serial aggregation loop of discoverPotentialMatches and in
  chooseSeeds. See rb3 docs/decomp/ghidra-bsim-perf-investigation-2026-06-10.md.

DESIGN (this patch — supersedes the empty-set prototype on branch
        bsim-perf-candidatecap)
  1. TOP-K OUTPUT TRUNCATION (BSimProgramCorrelatorMatching.findSimilarNodes):
     keep a bounded min-heap (PriorityQueue, size MAX_PAIRS_PER_QUERY = 32) of
     the best pairs per destination query, ranked by (similarity, confidence,
     source-node address). The true nearest neighbor(s) are preserved EXACTLY
     — unlike the empty-set cap, which dropped all candidates (including the
     best one) whenever a bin union exceeded 500. Downstream serial phases are
     bounded to O(n_dest x K) pairs.
     - Determinism: candidates iterate in TreeSet (address) order; PAIR_RANK is
       a strict total order with no ties within a query (each source node
       appears at most once), so the retained set is independent of scheduling.
  2. SECONDARY SAFETY CAP (BinningSystem.lookup, SAFETY_MAX_LOOKUP_CANDIDATES
     = 10000, was 500-and-return-empty in the prototype): top-K bounds the
     OUTPUT but not the per-query cosine-compare COST, which stays
     O(union_size). A union > 10000 (>25% of a 40k corpus co-binned) is
     pathologically generic — no confident match is possible — and the TreeSet
     union construction alone churns ~40 B/node of allocation per query. Only
     such pathological queries return empty; unions of 500..10000 (lossy under
     the prototype) now retain their exact top-K.
  3. OBSERVABILITY (the prototype capped silently; the original 70-min stall
     was undiagnosable from the log): after the aggregation loop, a Msg.info
     summary reports total discovered pairs, number of queries truncated to
     top-K, and number of queries rejected by the safety cap.

PARAMETERS
  MAX_PAIRS_PER_QUERY = 32      typical healthy queries pass 3-10 candidates,
                                so K=32 is never reached on well-behaved input
                                (bit-identical behavior to upstream there).
  SAFETY_MAX_LOOKUP_CANDIDATES = 10000
                                worst residual per-phase cost ~1e8 compares
                                (10k degenerate queries x 10k candidates),
                                parallel via the existing ConcurrentQ: tens of
                                seconds on 16 threads.

DOWNSTREAM-SEMANTICS ARGUMENT (why dropping rank>K pairs is safe)
  - chooseSeeds: a pair seeds only if unconflicted; dropped low-rank pairs can
    only REMOVE conflicts. For non-degenerate nodes (<= K pairs) nothing is
    dropped: identical behavior. For degenerate nodes, upstream's thousands of
    mutually-conflicting noise pairs mostly blocked seeding anyway; top-K keeps
    the strongest few, still subject to conflict checks and confThreshold.
  - doMatching/analyze: implication edges come from FunctionNode.associates
    (findEdge); only pairs below 32nd rank for their dest query lose edges —
    these are low-similarity edges that cannot win calculateBestNeighbor
    against retained higher-confidence edges of the same neighborhood.
  - Memory: <= n_dest x K pairs (65k x 32 ~ 2.1M, ~100 MB worst case) vs the
    unbounded 40k x 65k = 2.6e9 (impossible).

ESTIMATED WALL-CLOCK (degenerate 40k x 65k case, ~10k degenerate dest queries)
  upstream:            70+ min single-threaded stall (killed)
  top-K only:          ~4e8 parallel compares @ ~3 us (LSHCosineVector.compare
                       is an O(entries) sorted merge, ~hundreds of entries)
                       ~20 min CPU -> ~1.5-4 min wall on 16 threads, dominated
                       by compares + TreeSet-union allocation churn (~16 GB).
  top-K + safety cap:  residual <= 1e8 compares -> tens of seconds wall;
                       aggregation <= ~4M HashMap puts (~1-2 s);
                       chooseSeeds O(2M x rounds) (~tens of seconds).
  healthy same-ISA:    no change (neither bound is reached).

VERDICT: REPLACES the empty-set 500-cap prototype: strictly less lossy at
  comparable speed (seconds-to-tens-of-seconds for the degenerate case), and
  it logs what it did.

